tegrates compliance into workflow, not just logging practice? Possibly paradigm stretching (3). Could be slightly paradigm stretching (2) if still treats compliance as logging practice. The description says preserves scientific agility by eliminating retrospective documentation efforts during audits. That's weaving compliance into science.\n\nI think 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science). Yes.\n\nEvidence sentence: It integrates automated metadata harvesting from experiment trackers so compliance documentation is generated continuously alongside R&D rather than as a separate retrospective task.\n\nScore 3.\n\noriginality (1-4)\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, or surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nLiving technical documentation pipeline with knowledge graph is maybe interesting but not extremely novel? Automated metadata harvesting is somewhat common. Might be 2 = interesting. Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. The idea of living documentation pipeline is fairly known in MLOps. Probably not rare. I'd say 2 = interesting.\n\nEvidence: Automated sync of model versioning and validation results to regulatory records via knowledge graph is a recognizable MLOps automation rather than an imaginative surprise.\n\nScore 2.\n\neffectiveness (1-4)\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nDoes Living Technical Documentation Pipeline solve logging mandate? It addresses Recital 71 technical documentation, not Article 12 logs specifically? Recital 71 mentions technical documentation, automatic recording of events by logs. Proposal focuses on technical documentation pipeline synchronizing model versioning, dataset changes, validation results with regulatory-compliant record. It populates requirements outlined in Recital 71. It mentions automated metadata harvesting. Does it address logging mandate of Article 12? Article 12 is about automatic recording of events logs over lifetime. Technical documentation pipeline may help but not directly logs of use period, reference database etc. Might partially contribute.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (technical documentation part). But stated problem includes logging mandate. The proposal addresses Recital 71, not Article 12 logging details. It may be partial. So 3?\n\nCould be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Does it solve logging mandate? It says creates real-time dashboard for compliance auditors, preserves scientific agility by eliminating retrospective documentation efforts during audits. Might not cover Article 12(3) specific logs. Recital 71 mentions automatic recording of events by means of logs. Proposal mentions synchronizes model versioning... Not explicit logs of use period, reference database etc.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps with documentation but not full logging mandate).\n\nEvidence: The pipeline automates technical documentation per Recital 71 but does not explicitly generate the event logs for system use required by Article 12(1)-(3).\n\nScore 3.\n\napplicability (1-4)\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves an implied problem that is related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt clearly applies to meeting compliance documentation/logging. It's directly related. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal directly targets regulatory-compliant record-keeping for high-risk AI systems as mandated by Recital 71 and Article 12.\n\nScore 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4)\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nImplementing dynamic knowledge graph with automated metadata harvesting from experiment trackers is significant change, requires integration. Might be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 2 = significant change, expensive/difficult.\n\nProbably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. It's not trivial but doable for an R&D lab.\n\nEvidence: Building a knowledge graph pipeline and integrating experiment trackers requires engineering effort and data modeling but is feasible within an existing MLOps team.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4)\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nAutomated documentation for compliance is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Automating compliance documentation and providing auditor dashboards aligns with legal requirements and does not conflict with scientific norms.\n\nScore 4.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nOutput format? Probably JSON or list. Provide each.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1687\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nProceed.",
