still treats compliance as logging practice. Does it change relationship? It integrates compliance costs into early planning rather than retrospective. That is slightly stretching? Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nTying funding to compliance is acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nUpdating quarterly review criteria to include verification of logging capabilities. That's some administrative change. Probably easy to implement? Requires process change. Maybe 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Or 4 = easy? Might need audit infrastructure. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill tying funding to logging milestones solve logging mandate? It incentivizes but doesn't actually build logging tech. It ensures compliance costs integrated early. It may contribute but does not guarantee logs exist. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Could be 4? It would solve? No, it doesn't create logging capability itself. So 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly