formance is common problem. Not particularly ingenious. I'd say originality 2 = interesting.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem. 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). The proposal transforms regulatory burden into publishable scientific contribution while optimizing infrastructure. That is weaving logging into science, changing relationship. That's paradigm stretching = 3. Maybe slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice? It says transforms burden into publishable scientific contribution. That changes relationship. I think 3.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Problem: must decide how to respond: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal does research to implement high-resolution logging without degrading performance/storage costs. That directly applies to meeting logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Will compressed logging formats solve logging mandate? It helps implement logging but doesn't guarantee compliance with all Article 12 requirements (recording events relevant...). Might contribute but not fully solve? It addresses performance/storage cost barrier to logging. Could be partial solution. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, but only partial). Because logging still needs content relevance etc. I think effectiveness 3.\n\nLet's produce output.\n\nNeed ech