ms or sensibilities. Internal audit and stress testing is common, acceptable. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Periodic internal audit with simulated failures: some changes, maybe reasonably feasible measures? Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Is it easy at low cost? Might need resources. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Could argue 4? Let's pick 3 to be safe.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nAudit protocol is standard compliance practice. It's paradigm preserving? Might be slightly paradigm stretching introducing audit tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1? Standard internal audit is standard. I'd say 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. The description says protects reputation by proving trace failures before regulator. That's typical.\n\nMaybe 2. Hmm. The instrument wants paradigm_relatedness anchored to this problem. Audit protocol is a common compliance practice, not changing relationship. I think 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nRegulatory stress-testing audit is common mundane. So 1.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). Probably JSON? Not specified. Could be list.\n\nProbably output:\n\nid: P2067\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nLet's produce.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The protocol verifies existing logs but does not build the automated logging capability mandated by Article 12(1)-(3). Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: Verifying log capture directly addresses traceability and compliance required by the logging mandate. Score 4.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Internal audits and simulated failure testing are routine, lawful compliance practices with no normative offense. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Running periodic simulations and audits requires staff time and test infrastructure, representing reasonably feasible but not trivial changes. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal treats compliance as standard logging practice and leaves the lab's R&D workflow unchanged. Score 1.\n\nOriginality evidence: Periodic compliance audits and stress tests are common, mundane industry practice. Score 1.\n\nEnsure order listed: effectiveness, applicability, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality.\n\nReturn rating.\n\n",
