        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P2716.\n\nProposal: High-Risk Event Monitoring Dashboard. Real-time observability platform parses logs to identify operational risks or substantial modifications. Transforms raw logs into actionable compliance alerts. Develop heuristic and ML-based triggers scan system logs for anomalies linked to risk categories defined in Article 79. Fulfills post-market monitoring mandate of Article 12(2) by providing proactive alerts instead of reactive audits.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is dashboard to parse logs for risk. That's introducing tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's not changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally? It is just better monitoring. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds a dashboard and ML triggers but keeps compliance as a logging/monitoring task rather than redefining R&D.\n\noriginality (1-4): not expressed before AND ingenious... 4; unusual interesting shows some imagination 3; interesting 2; common mundane boring 1\n\nReal-time observability platform parsing logs for compliance alerts is fairly common. Might be interesting but not unusual? Probably common in industry. Could be 2 = interesting. Or 3? Heuristic and ML-based triggers scanning logs for anomalies linked to Article 79 risk categories is somewhat specific to AI Act. Might be unusual interesting. Hmm.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many would propose logging dashboard. That's common. I'd say 2 = interesting. Maybe 1? Common, mundane, boring. Real-time dashboard is fairly standard. I think originality 2 (interesting). Could be 3? Let's pick 2.\n\nimplementability (1-4): easy to implement low cost or non-radical changes =4; some changes reasonably feasible =3; significant change expensive difficult but not totally impossible=2; totally infeasible=1\n\nDeveloping observability platform with heuristic and ML triggers is significant change, expensive/difficult. Not easy. Probably 2? Or 3? Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be significant change. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Might require engineering effort but feasible. Let's say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): common strategies violate no norms=4; somewhat uncommon unusual strategies do not offend sensibilities=3; offends sensibilities somewhat=2; radically violates laws/sensibilities=1\n\nDashboard for monitoring is acceptable. No violation. Common strategy. So 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps partial). 2 = unreasonable unlikely to solve. 1 solves unrelated problem.\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce resources. Dashboard parses logs to identify risks. Does it meet logging mandate? Article 12 requires technically allow automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, logging capabilities enable