gination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nA formal trigger protocol with quarterly review board is fairly common compliance governance. Might be common/mundane? In AI labs, risk classification gates are somewhat common. I'd say interesting but not rare. Maybe 2 = interesting. Could be 3 unusual? Hmm.\n\nProbably 2: interesting. Let's pick 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nThis is acceptable, internal governance. Common strategies. No violation. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nEstablishing multidisciplinary review board quarterly evaluation is some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Easy? Requires resources. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nStated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal addresses when to apply logging. That's directly related. It aims to preserve agility by only applying logging when needed. So applicability 4? Does it solve logging mandate? It helps decide when logging applies, which is part of problem. Yes.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill this solve logging mandate? It may defer logging but does not actually implement logging. Might reduce burden but risk of misclassification. It contributes partially