al documentation current throughout system lifetime, an implied compliance burden linked to the record-keeping mandate.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\nWill it solve logging mandate? It addresses documentation not logs. So partial. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial). Or 2 unreasonable unlikely to solve problem because logging is separate. I'd say 3 contributes.\n\nEvidence: Keeping documentation current helps overall compliance but does not create the automatic event logs required by Article 12(1)-(3).\n\nNeed one sentence evidence then score.\n\nReturn rating echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nOrder listed: implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness.\n\nProvide output.\n\nProbably format JSON? It says return rating... maybe list.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P1489\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence each evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a pipeline that ingests Git commits, dataset hashes and hyperparameters into a dashboard requires integration work but uses existing tooling, making it reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automating documentation generation is a common compliance practice that raises no legal, social or business sensibility concerns. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The idea introduces new tools and living documents but still treats compliance as an internal documentation practice rather than changing the R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Linking version control metadata directly to a real-time compliance dashboard is unusual for labs and shows imaginative reuse of DevOps practices. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal addresses the implied need to keep technical documentation up to date under Recital 71, which is directly c