e logging mandate with privacy. Probably reasonable.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Does proposal do X to get Y where Y is meeting logging mandate? Yes, develop logging mechanisms that satisfy Article 12. So 4.\n\nOriginality: degree idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. Privacy-preserving audit logs using hashing/secure enclaves for AI Act compliance is perhaps unusual interesting but not extremely surprising? Could be 3. Might be common discussion. Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). Proposal transforms regulatory burden into competitive technical advantage, investigates tension between traceability and privacy. That's changing relationship? It weaves compliance into R&D for advantage. Might be paradigm stretching: changes relationship e.g., logging woven into science. Could be 3. Is it paradigm breaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It seems it changes relationship but not break? \"Transforms regulatory burden into competitive technical advantage\" suggests paradigm stretching. Maybe 3.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Dedicated R&D project to develop logging mechanisms... cryptographic hashing and secure enclaves... This is research, requires scarce implementation resources, significant change, expensive/difficult. Anchor: 4 easy low cost, 3 some changes reasonably feasible, 2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible, 1 totally infeasible. Developing new crypto logging system is significant change, expensive/difficult. So 2.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Privacy-preserving audit logs for regulators... seems acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms? Maybe somewhat uncommon but not offend. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Privacy preserving is acceptable, maybe 4. Could be 3 somewhat uncommon. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. I'd say 4.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence per subscale then score.\n\nProvide output format: echo id, for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence and <subscale>.\n\nLet's produce.\n\nEffectiveness_evidence: The proposal aims to create logging mechanisms that satisfy Article 12 while protecting data, which would directly enable compliance if the research succeeds. Score 3? Wait anchor 4 = will solve without regard for workability. If you could do it, it would solve main problem. If you could develop system, it would solve logging mandate. So maybe effectiveness 4? Because effectiveness disregards workability. It says reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve). The idea of privacy-preserving audit logs that satisfy Article 12 would solve problem. It's reasonable. So effectiveness 4.\n\nApplicability 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Using cryptographic hashing and secure enclaves to let regulators verify matches without exposing raw data is unusual for compliance logging. Score 3? Might be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: transforms regulatory burden into competitive advantage by weaving logging R&D into core research agenda, changing relationship between R&D and compliance. Score 3.\n\nImplementability: developing novel cryptographic logging with secure enclaves requires significant R&D effort, specialized expertise, and integration cost b