t logging mandate. That's directly related. Score 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms... Logging middleware is common. Immutable logging maybe privacy concerns? Captures input data - could be personal data. Might be concern. But overall acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Maybe somewhat uncommon? Sidecar architecture is fairly standard. I'd say 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Developing standardized immutable logging middleware integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines... That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost. Requires development of sidecar architecture across various projects. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 2 = significant change expensive or difficult. Hmm. Universal layer for all high-risk pipelines is non-trivial. Might be significant change. But proposal says minimizing friction. I think implementability = 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance.\n\nUniversal compliance logging layer is essentially standard compliance tool. Introduces new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? It captures input data without rewrite core model code - maybe changes relationship slightly. Probably paradigm preserving? Hmm.\n\nThe instrument says slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That fits. So score 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nUniversal compliance logging layer sidecar is fairly common idea in industry. Common, mundane. Could be 1. Or maybe 2 interesting? I think common middleware for compliance is common. Score 1.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... Provide lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2760\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: A universal immutable logging middleware that automatically records system events, timestamps and inputs would directly satisfy Article 12's mandate for automatic event logging and traceability. Score 4\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal explicitly targets the lab's decision of how to meet the Article 12 logging mandate with a mechanism to automate required record-keeping. Score 4\n\nacceptability_evidence: Implementing standardized audit logging via a sidecar is a common, legally aligned compliance practice that does not violate norms or sensibilities. Score 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building and integrating a standardized immutable sidecar layer across all high-risk pipelines requires non-trivial engineering changes but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The layer introduces a new logging tool and process but still treats compliance as separate logging practice rather than changing the lab's R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2\n\noriginality_evidence: A universal compliance logging middleware with sidecar capture is a common, mundane industry approach to regulatory logging. Score 1\n\nMake sure order.\n\nReturn as maybe JSON object.\n\nLet's output succinctly.",
