        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\nNeed rating for proposal P2568.\n\nProposal: Post-Market Risk Detection Dashboard. Analytics tool monitors system logs real time to identify performance drift or high-risk anomalies. Flags events that could constitute substantial modification. Develop alerting layer using statistical process control to scan operational logs for patterns indicating increased risk levels. Alerts trigger immediate compliance reviews. Fulfills Article 12(2) by facilitating proactive post-market monitoring and mitigating institutional liability.\n\nRate subscales:\n\nRelevance\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes dashboard solve logging mandate? It monitors logs, flags events for substantial modification, triggers compliance reviews. Logging mandate is to technically allow automatic recording of events (logs). Dashboard uses logs but doesn't create logging capability itself. It facilitates post-market monitoring per Article 12(2). Is that solving problem? Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposal helps comply with traceability/monitoring requirement, contributes to solution but does not provide logging itself. Might be partial solution.\n\nEffectiveness anchor: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, but only partial). Probably 3. It contributes to post-market monitoring but logging mandate is recording events; dashboard is downstream.\n\nEvidence sentence: The dashboard assumes logs already exist and adds analysis rather than providing the required automatic recording capability.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. 3 = solves implied problem related. Proposal addresses monitoring logs for risk detection, which is explicitly in Article 12(2)(b) facilitating post-market monitoring. That's directly related. Probably 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem: do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Facilitating post-market monitoring is part of mandate. Could be 4.\n\nMaybe applicability 4.\n\nNovelty\noriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious. 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nPost-market risk detection dashboard with SPC for AI logs is fairly common. Probably common/mundane? Might be interesting but not surprising. I'd say 2 = interesting. Or 1 = common, mundane. Many labs have monitoring dashboards. Possibly common. Let's choose 2? \"interesting\". Could be 1 common. Hmm.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (logging woven into science, new partners). 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance.\n\nDashboard is standard compliance tool, doesn't change R&D paradigm. It's just monitoring logs. So 1 or 2? Introduces new elements (analytics tool) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools... but still treats compliance as logging practice. That matches level 2. Could be 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. Hmm dashboard does change workflow? Might be slight.\n\nI think paradigm_relatedness = 2 (slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice)\n\nWorkability\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. 4 common strategies violate no norms. This is acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability: degree easily implemented. Analytics tool using statistical process control to scan logs... some changes or reasonably feasible measures? Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 2 significant change expensive/difficult. Developing real-time analytics dashboard is not trivial but feasible. Probably 3.\n\nNow output format: For each subscale, give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed echo id exactly.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2568\n\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n\napplicability_evidence...\napplicability:4\n\noriginality_evidence...\noriginality:2? Let's decide originality 2 = interesting. Could be 1 common mundane boring. I think monitoring dashboard is common. Might be 1. Hmm.\n\nLet's read anchor: 4 not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 interesting 1 common mundane boring. Post-market risk detection dashboard is fairly standard industry practice; not surprising. So 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nChoose originality 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence...\nparadigm_relatedness:2\n\nacceptability_evidence...\nacceptability:4\n\nimplementability_evidence...\nimplementability:3\n\nReturn JSON? Probably list.\n\nWill output lines.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProceed.",
