ne common metadata format that fulfills Article 12. That's socially/legal/politically acceptable. Common strategies? Not common but acceptable. I'd say 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Transparency is acceptable. Maybe 3.\n\nLet's pick 4? Acceptability: establishing lab as leader in scientific transparency increases global impact. Seems acceptable. Could be 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill a schema for lineage fulfill Article 12? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Lineage metadata is different. Schema may help documentation but does not provide automatic logs. Might contribute partially? It says fulfills Article 12 while facilitating cross-lab verification. If you could do it, would it solve main problem? Probably not fully; logging mandate is about event logs, not pedigree. So effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Or 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem.\n\nThe proposal claims fulfills Article 12. In reality lineage schema does not meet automatic logging requirement. But rating based on idea's claimed effectiveness? Should evaluate degree to which idea will solve problem. It may be ineffective because it addresses lineage not logs. Might be unreasonable. I think effectiveness = 2? Or 3?\n\nIt will contribute to traceability, but not automatic recording of events. Could be partial solution. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related. 3 solves