e risk classification is standard. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal preserves scientific ambition by ensuring not waste engineering hours on low-risk. That's just resource prioritization. Likely paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. I think 1.\n\nBut could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. It does introduce rubric triage. I'd lean 1 = paradigm preserving. Let's pick 1.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nProposal solves identified problem directly related? It applies rubric to triage projects allocating logging resources only where legally mandated. That directly addresses meeting logging mandate with limited resources. So solves identified problem directly related to stated problem. That's 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Will it solve? It helps ensure resources not wasted on low-risk, but logging mandate still requires logs for high-risk systems. Classification audit doesn't actually implement logging; just decides where to allocate. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because you still need to actually implement logging for high-risk. So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution... partial.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Systematic review process to categorize pipelines by risk level using rubric based on Annex III. That's some changes or reasonably feasible measures. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Might need effort but feasible. Could be 3.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. It's standard compliance triage. So 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Return rating echoing id exactly. Probably list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1008\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
