olate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nSome changes, or reasonably feasible measures? Integrating mandatory overhead into grant applications is some changes but reasonably feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Maybe 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Finance committee change may be moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nDefinition: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nBudgeting model changes relationship? It treats compliance as overhead cost, not woven into science. It's new element but still treats compliance as separate logging practice. Maybe slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (funding process) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nCommon mundane boring? Budget earmarking for compliance is common. Might be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting. Hmm experienced evaluator expect labs to give responses like allocate budget. Might be fairly common. I'd say 1 common mundane.\n\nCheck definitions: originality 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 interesting. 1 common mundane boring.\n\nEarmarking compliance overhead is pretty standard practice in regulated industries. So 1.\n\nProvi