e boring. Unified logging library is fairly common practice in labs for compliance. Probably common, mundane. So originality 1? Might be 2 = interesting. I'd say common practice: standardized logging library is standard engineering. So 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nProposal is standard compliance logging practice, modular API. That's paradigm preserving? It introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. The description says minimizes redundant engineering effort. That is slight stretch? Might be paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Hmm.\n\nThe anchored levels: 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; the lab's way of working is unchanged. Actually they introduce a unified framework, which changes workflow somewhat (researchers must call functions). That's maybe slight change. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... ) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nChoose paradigm_relatedness 2.\n\nNeed output format. Probably JSON with id and scores? Instruction says Return rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Might be just lines.\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P1641