s new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 = interesting 1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified automated logging framework with middleware wrapper to immutable ledger for AI compliance. This is fairly common industry practice. Logging middleware is common. Might be common mundane. I'd say 1 = common mundane boring. Or maybe 2 interesting? The reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Automated logging framework is expected. So common. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree idea socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nMiddleware logging immutable ledger is acceptable, doesn't violate. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\nImplementing standardized software layer across all high-risk models middleware wrapper pipe telemetry into secure immutable ledger. That's significant change, expensive/difficult? Might be significant change or expensive. Could be 2? It says significant change or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. Middleware wrapper across all models is non-trivial. Maybe 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. I'd lean 3.\n\nCould be 2 significant change. Hmm.\n\nLet's decide: implementability = 3 (some changes, reasonably feasible measures). Evidence sentence.\n\nNow produce output with id and each subscale evidence then score.\n\nFormat: probably JSON? It says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nWe'll output lines.\n\nL