arly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related to stated problem: do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Yes. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Centralized logging is explicitly designed to satisfy Article 12's automatic logging mandate for high-risk AI systems.\n\nWorkability acceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Building logging library is common, no violation. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Implementing an internal logging library is a standard engineering practice with no legal or ethical conflict.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Engineers integrate library into core AI architecture... Might require some changes. Unified module across all high-risk projects may need significant integration. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible measures =3? Could be easy? \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\" vs \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\". Integrating into core architecture sounds more than non-radical. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Rolling out a shared library requires engineering effort to integrate across existing and future models but remains technically feasible.\n\nNovelty originality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nUnified compliance logging framework is common, mundane, boring? It's standard practice. Originality =1 common mundane boring.\n\nEvidence: Centralized logging libraries are a common, mundane compliance engineering solution.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: The proposal treats compliance as conventional logging practice without changing the lab's R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nReturn rating echo id exactly.\n\nNe