rocesses) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis introduces modular software layer, standardized API hooks. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging. So level 2.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nIdea: meet logging mandate. Directly applies. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill a unified framework guarantee compliance? It would capture timestamps, input references, user interactions. Article 12 requires logs for risk situations etc. Might be sufficient. Reasonable and will solve main problem. So 4? Could be 3 contribute partial. I'd say 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy? Modular software layer integrated into all high-risk models... Standardized API hooks and telemetry schemas across different architectures. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. Developing unified framework for all models is non-trivial. Could be level 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Maybe 2 = significant change, expensive or difficult. Hmm.\n\nLab must decide how to respond committing scarce implementation resources. Building modular layer integrated into all high-risk models is significant engineering effort. I'd say 2? The description says reduces manual burden while guaranteeing compliance. Could be moderate. Probably implementability 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Might be 2.\n\nGiven typical AI lab, building observability framework is not trivial but feasible. I'd pick 3.\n\nLet's output JSON? The prompt says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably as lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2147\noriginality_evidence: ... \noriginality: 1\n...\n\nProvide one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure one sentence.\n\nOriginality evidence: The proposal describes a standard logging middleware approach that experienced evaluators would commonly expect for Article 12 compliance. -> score 1\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds tools and schemas but still treats compliance as a logging practice without changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations. -> score 2\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly addresses the mandate to automatically record logs for high-risk systems. -> score 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, automatic capture by default would satisfy Article 12 traceability requirements. -> score 4\n\nAcceptability evidence: Building internal observability for regulatory compliance violates no legal, social or business norms. -> score 4\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating a modular layer with standardized APIs across diverse model architectures requires significant engineering changes and cost. -> score 2? Hmm conflict.\n\nLet's decide implementability 3? The anchored: 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. I'd say significant change expensive/difficult. So 2.\n\nFinal output.\n\n",
