s. Research initiative doesn't immediately meet logging mandate. Might be ineffective? It says directly addressing legal requirements for monitoring. Hmm.\n\nThe instrument: effectiveness is degree idea will solve problem, without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). If you could do it, would it solve? Interpretable logs would help monitoring. Could solve. But it's research not immediate implementation. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes logging mandate for monitoring high-risk systems. So 4? It solves identified problem directly related: do interpretable logs to meet monitoring requirement. Might be 4.\n\nLet's decide scores:\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The initiative reframes compliance logging as a research program on AI observability, weaving logging into the lab’s science. => score 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Creating human-comprehensible risk indicators from raw logs via novel aggregation and anomaly detection is unusual and shows imagination for this regulatory context. => score 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: A research initiative to improve interpretability and post-market monitoring violates no legal, social or business norms. => score 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Launching a new research stream with novel methods and publications requires significant effort and time beyond routine logging implementation. => score 2\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If realized, interpretable logs would help identify risks and modifications, contributing to traceability and post-market monitoring required by Article 12. => score 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal targets automatic event recording and monitoring of high-risk systems as mandated by Article 12 and Recital 71. => score 4\n\nReturn JSON? The prompt says return rating echoing id exac