tion\n2 interesting\n1 common mundane boring\n\nClassification audit protocol is common, mundane. Score 1.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 solves an identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 intervention not stated...\n\nProblem: lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal applies classification to apply correct record-keeping standards. That's directly related to meeting logging mandate. So applicability 4? Solves identified problem that is directly related: need to know which systems need logs. Yes. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\n2 unreasonable...\n1 solves unrelated problem\n\nWill classification ensure record-keeping capabilities? It identifies which requirements apply, prevents gaps. It contributes but does not itself implement logging. It's partial solution. Might be 3. Because solving logging mandate requires actual logging implementation, not just classification. So contributes but partial. Score 3.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change, expensive...\n1 totally infeasible\n\nPre-development checklist and quarterly reviews by compliance office and lead scientists. Reasonably feasible. Some changes. Score 3? Could be 4 easy? Requires compliance office time. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 4 easy to implement at low cost? Checklist is low cost. Maybe 4. Hmm \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" vs \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\". Checklist and quarterly reviews is somewhat more than non-radical. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (